55. Thread Pool Example

Suppose your service calls three external APIs.

Bad:

java
payment();
inventory();
notification();

Sequential:

200ms
+
300ms
+
200ms

= 700ms

Potentially parallel:

java
CompletableFuture<Payment> payment =
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                this::payment);

CompletableFuture<Inventory> inventory =
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                this::inventory);

CompletableFuture.allOf(
        payment,
        inventory
).join();

But don't use the common ForkJoinPool blindly in an enterprise service.

Use a controlled executor:

java
@Bean
public ExecutorService applicationExecutor() {

    return Executors.newFixedThreadPool(20);
}

and explicitly manage lifecycle/configuration in production.


56. Connection Pooling

For PostgreSQL, you don't want:

Request
 ↓
Create DB connection
 ↓
Query
 ↓
Close

every time.

Use a connection pool such as HikariCP:

Hikari Pool

Connection 1
Connection 2
Connection 3
...
Connection 20

Request gets:

connection
   ↓
query
   ↓
return connection

Important settings include:

properties
spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=20
spring.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=5
spring.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout=3000

Don't assume "more connections = more performance." The database itself becomes the bottleneck if you oversize the pool.


